Program in execution is process

Context of a process : Every process has its own context means updating what process has done upto means Register values,Program state,Program Counter,etc. Special Data Structure which keeps track of context is Struct. PCB is a struct (Process Control Block)

system call is way to request a service from the operating system's kernel.
2 Privilege modes - User mode ( Non-Privileged Mode ) - Ring 3 and Kernel mode ( Privileged Mode ) - Ring 0, Ring 1, Ring 2
Each system call has a unique number associated to it and OS has a system call table which maps this numbers to a particular functions
So when user invokes a system call with number and parameters associated with system call in the register(RAX - cpu register) then syscall is executed.
Parameters are kept in a stack rather than a register as parameters can be huge.

function call - executed in user mode, has programs own memory space, fast.
system call - executed in kernel mode, has protected kernel address space, slow due to context switching.

Thread is the smallest unit of execution in OS.
One process can have multiple threads.
Communication in process is done using IPC Inter Process Communication and communication in thread is fast as it can directly read/write to shared variables.
Threads have their own Program counter, Register, Stack
Threads should not share stack and registers and can share data, code, files, heap.
All the threads are part of same address space

Types of threads :
User level threads -  threads made using function calls, Managed entirely in user space
kernel level threads - threads made using system call,Managed, scheduled, and created directly by the operating system kernel using system call
For each thread there is a Thread Control block (TCB) or Process Control Block (PCB)

User-level threads (ULTs) are threads of execution that are managed entirely by user-space thread libraries and from kernel's pov application is just single-threaded process.
From user thread pov kernel can only see the processes p1,p2,p3 not the threads inside the processes but from kernel thread pov kernel can see the threads associated with processes p1,p2,p3

Context Switch : Saving the state of processes before switching to another process and restoring original process state when switching back.

File System in UNIX:

sector - unit of storage 
usually sector size is 4KB (depends upon software windows,mac,unix,etc)

metadata of a file or directory is stored in a data structure called inode 
for every file or directory we have 1 inode
every inode has 1 inode number

inode of a file contains - metadata , data block pointer ( pointer where actual data blockes are there in stirage device) doesn't contain file name or actual file content.
inode of a directory contains - metadata same as any file only i.e permissions, timestamps, etc and data blocks ( pointer to the inode number of files). Data block of the directory contains inode table 

Disk contains : some part has inode numbers and max part contains actual data

Allocating Disk Space to files :
1) Contiguous allocation - with some specific length allocated for a sector/block file is placed in the disk. Inode contains starting location of the block and length. Sequential access is faster and random access for a block is also faster (address divided by block size). File size can't be grown easily.
2) Extent based allocation - multiple contiguous region for a file
3) Linked allocation - all files in block are stored in linked list. Each block contains pointer to the first block in the file.The directory stores a pointer to the starting and ending blocks. Slow sequential access and random access also is slow. File size can be grown easily.
4) Variation FAT allocation - Bring all the pointers of linked lists(nodes) to a single place in a file allocation table (FAT). There will be one entry of every sector in FAT
